msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:88
msgid "Why does Redox have unsafe Rust code?"
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:90
msgid ""
"In some cases we must use `unsafe` declarations to allow some low-level "
"tasks, for example at certain parts in the kernel and drivers, these unsafe "
"parts are generally wrapped with a safe interface."
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:92
msgid "These are the cases where unsafe Rust is mandatory:"
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:94
msgid ""
"Implementing a foreign function interface (FFI) (for example the relibc API)"
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:95
msgid ""
"Working with system calls directly (you should use `libredox`, `relibc` or "
"Rust `libstd` library instead of `redox_syscall`)"
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:96
msgid "Creating or managing processes and threads"
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:97
msgid "Working with memory mapping and stack allocation"
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:98
msgid "Working with hardware devices"
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:100
msgid ""
"It is an important goal for Redox to minimize the amount of `unsafe` "
"declared Rust code. If you want to use unsafe Rust code on Redox anywhere "
"other than interfacing with system calls, ask for Jeremy Soller's approval "
"before."
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:102
msgid ""
"Unsafe Rust still has most of the compiler verification and allows some safe "
"Rust syntax usage, thus it is still more safe than C and C++."
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:104
msgid "Read the following pages to learn more about Unsafe Rust:"
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:106
msgid "https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch20-01-unsafe-rust.html"
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:107
msgid "https://doc.rust-lang.org/nomicon/meet-safe-and-unsafe.html"
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:109
msgid "Why does Redox have Assembly code?"
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:111
msgid ""
"[Assembly](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Assembly_language) is the core of "
"low-level because it's a CPU-specific programming language and deals with "
"things that aren't possible or feasible to do in high-level languages like "
"Rust."
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:113
msgid ""
"Sometimes required or preferred for accessing hardware, or for carefully "
"optimized hot spots."
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:115
msgid "Reasons to use Assembly instead of Rust:"
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:117
msgid "Deal with low-level things (those that can't be handled by Rust)"
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:118
msgid "Writing constant time algorithms for cryptography"
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:119
msgid "Optimizations"
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:121
msgid "Places where Assembly is used:"
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:123
msgid ""
"`kernel` - Interrupt and system call entry routines, context switching, "
"special CPU instructions and registers"
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:124
msgid "`drivers` - Port IO need special instructions (x86_64)"
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:125
msgid "`relibc` - Some parts of the C runtime"
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:127
msgid "Why does Redox do cross-compilation?"
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:129
msgid ""
"[Cross-compilation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cross_compiler) is when "
"you build a program or library from one CPU architecture to another CPU "
"architecture or one operating system to another operating system, but it "
"requires more configuration than native compilation."
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:131
msgid "Read some of the reasons below:"
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:133
msgid ""
"When developing a new operating system you can't build applications inside "
"of it because the system interfaces are premature. Thus you need to build "
"the applications from your host system to the new OS and transfer the "
"binaries to the filesystem of the new OS."
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:134
msgid ""
"Cross-compilation reduces the porting requirements because you don't need to "
"support the compiler of the program's programming language, the program's "
"build system and build tools. You just need to port the programming language "
"standard library (if used), program libraries or the program source code "
"(dependency-free)."
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:135
msgid ""
"Some developers prefer to develop from other operating systems like Linux, "
"MacOS, FreeBSD or Windows, the same applies for Linux where some developers "
"write code on MacOS and test their kernel builds in a virtual machine "
"(mostly QEMU) or real hardware."
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:137
msgid ""
"(Interpreted applications and scripts don't need cross-compilation but the "
"programming language's interpreter or possible compiled dependencies needs "
"to be ported and cross-compiled to Redox)"
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:139
msgid "When relibc ABI breaks?"
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:141
msgid "When dynamically linked (most cases) it breaks when:"
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:143
msgid "A function is renamed"
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:144
msgid "A function is removed"
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:145
msgid "Any function with `extern C` have their arguments changed"
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:146
msgid "The size or alignment of any struct is changed"
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:148
msgid ""
"New functions don't break ABI but are backwards-incompatible (programs "
"compiled to previous relibc API can't use them)."
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:150
msgid "When statically linked, the kernel ABI can't break."
msgstr ""

