msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:464
msgid "What is a scheme?"
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:466
msgid "Read the [Schemes and Resources](./schemes-resources.md) page."
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:468
msgid "When does a regular program need to use a scheme?"
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:470
msgid ""
"Most schemes are used internally by system components or relibc, you don't "
"need to access them directly. One exception is the pseudoterminal for your "
"command window, which is accessed using the value of `$TTY`, which might "
"have a value of e.g. `pty:18`. Some low-level graphics programming might "
"require you to access your display, which might have a value of e.g. "
"`display:3`"
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:472
msgid "When would I write a program to implement a scheme?"
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:474
msgid ""
"If you are implementing a kernel service, userspace service or a device "
"driver."
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:476
msgid "How do I use a scheme for sandboxing a program?"
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:478
msgid ""
"The [contain](https://gitlab.redox-os.org/redox-os/contain) program provides "
"a partial implementation of sandboxing using schemes and namespaces."
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:480
msgid "How can I see all userspace schemes?"
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:482
msgid "Read the [Userspace Schemes](./schemes.md#userspace-schemes) section."
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:484
msgid "How can I see all kernel schemes?"
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:486
msgid "Read the [Kernel Schemes](./schemes.md#kernel-schemes) section."
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:488
msgid "What is the difference between kernel and userspace schemes?"
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:490
msgid ""
"Read the [Kernel vs Userspace Schemes](./schemes.md#kernel-vs-userspace-"
"schemes) section."
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:492
msgid "How does a userspace daemon provide file-like services?"
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:494
msgid ""
"When a regular program calls `open`, `read`, `write`, etc. on a file-like "
"resource, the kernel translates that to a message of type `syscall::data::"
"Packet`, describing the file operation, and makes it available for reading "
"on the appropriate daemon's scheme file descriptor. See the [Providing A "
"Scheme](./scheme-operation.md#providing-a-scheme) section for more "
"information."
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:496
msgid "How are the system calls used by userspace daemons?"
msgstr ""

#: src/developer-faq.md:498
msgid ""
"All userspace daemons use the system calls through [relibc](https://gitlab."
"redox-os.org/redox-os/relibc) like any normal program."
msgstr ""

